fore me  standing on the shoulders of giants in  this regard  so the contemporary thought would be  again we don't limit  what we think of these three words to be  we're looking at them in much more  expansive  interpretations and i would encourage  you to look at the nc3r  site to see this so for veterinary  medicine  we are doing research and it is  scientific research  and it's looking at all kinds of items  related to disease  therapies how to abide by refinements  looking into animal health knowing about  their behavior  this is all a very rapidly expanding  science and we translate this into  practical information that's critical  to continue to minimize pain and  suffering and also ensure  robustness and reproducibility for  experiments  so i just want to point out that this in  and of itself the idea  of veterinary management of animals will  continue to be  a massive area of discovery  there are a myriad of veterinary  journals animal science journals  professional seminars etc  that are looking at this specifically  and  these are evidence-based improvements  they are they are based in fact  we are in a very unique time as we all  know  and the pandemic itself offers  a great example for how to think about  animals  you know the idea that we need the right  model for the right question  it's the scientific process itself  that's going to drive our ability to  ultimately reduce animal numbers in  certain disciplines  for example the covid vaccine that a lot  of people have said oh well we didn't  need animals for that we just got it out  into humans as quickly as possible  they haven't got all of the data they  haven't got the information  that a lot of the work was done in  animals for sars  several years ago and now has been able  to be reused and adapted more quickly  from animal work that was done in the  past so  we do have an ethical obligation more  than ever to keep driving science  forward  and create new knowledge i think this  still goes back to  principle number two from the 80s  procedures should consider relevance to  human and animal health  advancement of knowledge and the good of  society  just mentioning here the harm benefit  assessment is it underlies everything  that we do  so a couple of quick things as we finish  up there has been  a pandemic pet proliferation there's a  lot of alliteration there  but i thought it was really important  this article just was shown out  a week ago in the michigan veterinary  medical association  and i know this personally i have not  acquired new pets but i have so many  friends and colleagues  that have and so when we think about  this we are not reducing animal numbers  we are creating animal numbers um not  only adoptions from shelters  adoptions from research institutions but  also from breeders  um this is what's happening because  science shows  that pets and humans  have incredible health benefits when  there are times of stress  and we've known this but again this is a  field of study that involves animals  social sciences and human work  so when we think about proliferation it  was timely then that  just last week in the last couple of  weeks there's been a big splash  about how many animals are too many um  you know what does it depend on is there  societal benefit to one but not another  and who gets to decide these things  so i wanted to just touch on the fact  that this article came out basically  stating  that um there's a hundred million  animals um it was based on a paper that  was  put together by dr larry caboni who is a  lab animal  veterinarian he made some estimates  based on data that he was able to get  through  conversations with colleagues and  through some foia requests  where he estimated that there were more  than 100 million mice and rats being  used in labs today  he acknowledged it was just an estimate  but from that  there were um in david grimm who's a  science writer  um he sort of pushed and i think maybe  glossed over  some of the important statements that dr  carboni placed into his publication  so he acknowledged that it was just an  estimate and he also stated  that that was about 99 of all the lab  animals were  rodent species he also pointed out that  there is a system of  inspections in place that we do  whistleblower investigations that there  is coverage and oversight for rodents in  public and private institutions and  those of us within the animal  biomedical community and who participate  in all of these activities  are well aware of these oversight  activities that i discussed  the iac review the vet consult  justification of animal numbers and  alternative considerations  but unfortunately in this summary i felt  like dr  carboni was glossed over by mr grimm on  these important statements  and kind of tilted the angle to animal  protectionists  there wasn't the facts there wasn't  accurate data everything was based on  estimations and he did  acquire a quote from the president of  alternatives to research and development  foundation  which their goal is to reduce the number  of animals in labs and find replacements  and her statement was the figures are  troubling because mice and rats aren't  covered by the animal welfare act  when we know they're covered by other  regulations  and her quote was if the numbers are  anywhere near correct the amount of pain  and suffering that's occurring in these  animals  acceptable myself and others find this  to be  like really sort of reckless  in a statement there was no information  provided as to the care and well-being  the models that these animals were used  for the oversights  the interventions so i just want to  echo my colleague dr allison bennett who  was also quoted in this science mag  article that she also agreed it was a  pretty disappointing analysis  we have to think about things more  broadly and we have to have more  discussion about them  so thinking about that if we were to  reduce animals  another area in our case studies for now  includes what type of rodent we're using  so the microbiome has been a very hot  topic  the microbiome is basically the full set  of flora that we have in our  gastrointestinal tracts and the  interaction between them  and our brain and other systems and so  wild mice  which normally many would consider to be  pests  there are whole businesses built around  getting rid of invasions of  wild mice and wild rats in our  communities and our homes and our farms  now there's going to be more of them  used in research analyses  so does that type of rodent what we use  does that matter to society  this will be an interesting and ethical  discussion ahead as well  and the last thing that i wanted to  point out which i just thought was a  really just a super cool article  was from the new yorker last month um it  talks about  and i think is a good example of the  progressive use of three of the three  r's  so crispr is commented upon here i  mentioned that um  that this is a 2020 nobel prize winner  as a technology  jennifer dunno was one of the recipients  from uc berkeley of the prize  as she's one of the developers and she  calls it a way to rewrite the very  molecules of life in which we live  so interestingly and briefly this  article  talks about how crispr has been used to  manipulate  the cane toad which is overtaking the  continent of australia  it was introduced to basically try to  control pests like bugs and things like  that  but evolution pushed quickly and these  cane toads grew very large there are  six plus pounds each and they have just  taken over  so gene editing using crispr is now  um available to change the skin tone of  the toads so that you can identify which  ones have been manipulated  you can also impact the fact that each  toad can give birth  or sorry it can lay like 1.5 million  eggs  explains the proliferation the problem  is that if we manipulate  the gel layer on the outside of the eggs  they may not be able to be fertilized  when these toads get big enough they  will eat anything and everything that's  smaller than them  including other smaller cane toads  snakes alligators etc  so in this idea of the progressive 3rds  we need to think about reduction  using science to reduce the number of  cane toads to allow the proliferation of  species that otherwise  are bordering on the brink of extinction  so the goals here  are to enhance biodiversity through  genetic rescue  and just kind of a fun a fun story  it's social benefit versus animal  welfare so these are the discussions we  continue to have  so for the future three r's i would  challenge us to think we're going to  continue to have more principles that  come into play as people talk about it  further  and we're going to have more species  like the cane toad that we have to  discuss we are all advocates  in this process every single one of us  if we're talking about it  we have an advocacy investment and so  what i just wanted to point out about  the importance of that  is that we want to take what we've  discussed here and make it better if we  can there are so many advocacy  organizations i'm going to list a lot of  them at the end of the talk  and we need to have this advocacy to  safeguard the future of biomedical and  veterinary research  involving animal models it would be  naive to assert that the only advocates  for animals are those in the animal  rights camp or in the animal protection  groups  our whole community drives momentum  towards animal advocacy  and we have to continue to do that for  beneficial outcomes  one comment i think many of you may be  aware that there is a  working group that's ongoing right now  i've had the privilege to be involved  with that through the nih  um this was charged as a group in  the late 2019 to come together and talk  about  assessing and making recommendations for  reproducibility and the rigor of animal  research  specifically to improve experimental  design optimize translatability  enhance training and increase  transparency these same themes  that i have been talking about and  showing you have been in  discussion since the 1970s  so we're continuing to look at gaps the  report should be coming out in june  of 2021 here we're still having meetings  about it and it will be something to  to look at on the horizon so the three  r's  are a masterpiece just as i'm showing  you here  these are self-portraits that van gogh  did  initially they were not regarded as  anything special and over time  they have become viewed as masterpieces  and what i wanted to show you here was  the progression in his work so from left  to right  um van gogh started off as kind of a  country living in the country with his  family and then he moved to paris and he  finally paints  himself as as an artist you can  appreciate that there's a change over  time and it is a triptych  again like our three r's the three r's  have remained ubiquitous in large part  because of their benefit to the animals  but they also have importance for  us as well the stakeholders so here's  the  is the icon that i've put together just  to express  how we can reconsider the foundational  or classic 3rs  with different interpretations very much  combining them with the synergy of other  ethical tools of analysis  and validity assessments that we've  talked about all of these are going to  be essential in the ongoing  intricate considerations of the  appropriate and future  animal use through artistic terms the  three r's themselves are also a  masterpiece  and they're a work in progress and i  hope we can all appreciate that  and continue the discussion so with that  i'd like to thank  the organizers for the lab roots  conference i had a lot of really  fascinating discussions and we'll  continue to do so with the colleagues  here from  lab animal medicine and preminer if you  want to take any arts classes i thought  i should just do a little plug for the  barnes museum  and all of the advocacy groups are  listed on the right  that i many of which i'm a part of and  many of which i've learned from  so thank you very much and i really hope  you enjoy the rest of the sessions


